import
	../html
	../page-template

faq html()
	page-template "faq.html", "FAQ",
		main: faq-main

-faq-main html[]()
	- section "How do I disable overflow checking?",
		- p quote
			Functions like {std-function "keen/numbers", "+"} will always check for overflow
			(except for {float} inputs). There's no way to globally disable this.
			{br}
			To allow overflow in a specific case, use functions like {std-function "keen/bits", "wrap-add"}.
		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/bits

			main void()
				log wrap-add::nat8 255, 1

	- section "How do I disable {assert_}s?",
		- p quote
			Asserts can't be globally disabled.
			{br}
			If there are certain expensive asserts in your program that you might want to turn off,
			you will have to come up with a way to make them conditional.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				if expensive-asserts
					assert every 1 .. 100, it < 100

			expensive-asserts bool() = true

	- section "How do I do nothing?",
		- p "{parens} is the empty {void} value."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				()

	- section "How do I parse JSON or Kid files?",
		- p "Parsing functions are in {std-module "keen/parse"}."
		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/parse

			main void()
				log parse json, "[1, 2]"

	- section "How do I test if a string is a substring of another?",
		- p quote
			There are often not string-specific functions when a generic collection function will work.
			In this case, you can use {std-function "keen/col", "contains-seq"} to test for a substring.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log contains-seq "coworker", "cow"

	- section "Why are {fn-log} and {fn-print} different?",
		- p quote
			{fn-log} is allowed anywhere.
			This only writes to stderr and is not considered the program's output,
			so it isn't considered a true side effect.
			{fn-print} is the program's actual output and so is considered a true side effect.

	- section "Why does the standard library not use interfaces or contexts?",
		- p quote
			The standard library is used by all Keen programs,
			but the best interfaces to use depends on specific circumstances.
			{br}
			So instead, the standard library just defines {global_} functions and leaves abstraction to the programmer.

	- section "Why can't I compare mutable values with {fn-equal}?",
		- p quote
			To avoid confusion.
			It's unclear whether {fn-equal} on mutable values should compare the contents or the identity.
			{br}
			To compare the identity, use {fn-reference-equal}.
			(This is {unsafe} in general, but can be {trusted_} for mutable values.)
			{br}
			To compare the contents, convert to immutable values and compare those.
		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/unsafe

			main void()
				a nat mut[] = 1, 2
				b nat mut[] = 1, 2
				log trusted reference-equal a, b
				log a.to::nat[] == b.to::nat[]

	- section "Why are {enum_} and {flags_} types different?",
		- p quote
			In Keen, an {enum_} type is guaranteed to have one of the listed values.
			A {flags_} type can have any combination of values.

	- section "Why can't I use a mutable type as a map key?",
		- p quote
			If you mutated the object, its hash could change, making it unretrievable.
			{br}
			If you need to associate some data with mutable values (and can't just add it as a field),
			you could give the values an identity property and key the map by the identity.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				the identity-provider = 0,
				values my-type[] = (64,), (151,)
				map string[nat] = (values[0].id, "a"), (values[1].id, "b")
				for v in values
					log v.value, map[v.id]

			identity-provider record mut
				-last-id mut nat
			get-next-id nat() identity-provider
				the::identity-provider.last-id +:= 1
				the::identity-provider.last-id

			my-type record mut
				id nat
				value mut nat
			new my-type(value nat) identity-provider
				get-next-id, value
